# Epistemic Governance for Agent Memory
# PAPER1

v2.2 — adds execution discipline (§5.11), scale-appropriate competence in reference disagreement (§5.8), two missing terms in the compression bound (§7.2), correction latency as a health metric (§11.4), and one open problem that was in no paper (§13.11).
1. The Problem
Persistent memory systems for LLM agents have converged on a common architecture: extract claims from interaction, store them in a graph or vector index alongside embeddings, retrieve them on demand. These systems answer where knowledge should be stored. They do not answer what a claim's epistemic status is — what earns durable storage, what yields to better evidence, what is retired when it stops mattering, and how much standing a claim deserves given what stands behind it.
That second question is the subject of this proposal. Current systems address it with heuristics — recency, similarity, occasional staleness marking — or not at all.
Two lines of evidence that the gap costs something.
Cascade propagation fails measurably. When a fact changes, every claim licensed by that fact may need to change with it. In manuscript-revision settings, where this can be measured directly against a controlled fact graph, five LLM editing systems span Edit-Ripple Adherence of roughly 0.15–0.71, with even the strongest missing about 30% of required downstream updates. The parameter-editing literature (§2.3) reports the same class of failure from a different angle — edits that succeed locally while dependent facts remain stale — though without a single comparable headline metric. These are adjacent threads, not one: manuscript revision measures a model rewriting text, parameter editing measures a model's internal beliefs. Both indicate the same absent capability.
Growth is structurally unbounded. The argument here is asymptotic rather than empirical, which makes it stronger. Support-edge storage is O(claims × mean fan-out), worst case O(n²); provenance is O(claims × sources per claim); regression-set closure over uncertain edges is O(V·E) to precompute. Nothing in an accumulate-and-link architecture reduces any of these. Published footprint measurements vary by more than fivefold across systems and are heavily design-dependent, too sensitive to design choices to anchor a claim; the asymptotics are not.
The diagnosis this proposal rests on. Both problems trace to one absence. A system that does not represent what depends on what cannot propagate an update to its consequences, cannot determine what an update would break, and cannot compress redundant structure because it cannot identify redundancy.
1.1 The central falsifiable claim
Beyond the two measured failures above, this proposal makes one diagnostic bet that is testable on existing models with no new architecture:
A system absorbing information as fact-additions when the situation demands reorganizing the framework that interprets those facts will degrade in a way distinguishable from ordinary staleness. Each update looks locally acceptable while global structure deteriorates, because the required reorganization never happens.
T0.2 tests this and can end a substantial part of the program. The claim is stated here rather than alongside its mechanism because the mechanism (§8.2) is optional and the claim is not — if the degradation ratio comes back near 1.0, the routing taxonomy collapses to binary and several sections below lose their motivation.
1.2 Terminology
"Claim" means an individuable behavioral abstraction — a regularity in system behavior that can be identified, tracked, and evaluated as a unit. It does not mean a parameter location, a stored proposition, or a symbolic record. In a retrieval store, a claim is provisionally operationalized as a cluster of entries that co-activate above threshold; whether such clusters correspond to stable behavioral units is what T0.1 tests, and it is the first and highest-severity experiment in this proposal's own build order.
"Protection" is a resistance weight attached to a claim, not a physical partition. Higher protection means an update touching that claim requires more evidence and more validation to proceed.
"Standing" is a claim's position in the store — its protection weight, its decay resistance, its eligibility to be cited as support by other claims. Standing is distinct from evidence, denominated differently, and issued by the governance layer rather than supplied by the world. §5.3 states the conditions under which issuing it is legitimate.
1.3 Scope
This proposal addresses declarative knowledge acquired after deployment, in single-agent systems with explicit retrieval.
Declarative only. Procedural knowledge — reasoning strategies, tool-use patterns, formatting habits — has no evidence trail, no independently corroborating instances, and no bounded set of downstream dependents, so the machinery here has nothing to attach to. This boundary is conceptually clean but operationally leaky: systems like MemGPT routinely extract habits and rules as structured notes, which will land in the declarative store regardless of scope. The practical requirement is a filter that detects procedural content and declines to govern it, and no such filter is specified here.
Post-deployment only. §3.3 states the cost rather than minimizing it, and proposes a partial route across the boundary.
Single-agent only. The exclusion is not merely a deferral of distributed-consistency problems. A panel of models supplies reduction of single-model idiosyncrasy in adjudication, which is a real benefit, and does not supply external constraint, because a channel between peers carries no setpoint. What makes the reference layer (§5.8) constraining is not that it originates elsewhere but that it terminates in a format change the system cannot run backwards; another model of the same kind is not such a channel, however differently trained. A model with retrieval or tool access is a partial exception — it carries a borrowed transductive limb — but the constraint it then supplies is inherited from whatever its tools terminate in, which is the constraint the reference layer already provides at lower cost. §8.6 specifies a multi-agent mechanism for the one disposition class where single-model idiosyncrasy is unrecoverable, and marks it as out of scope for the experiments here.
Explicit retrieval required. Forced by a measurement constraint (§3.2), not chosen.
2. Related Work
2.1 Truth maintenance (1977–1990s)
The nearest antecedent is roughly forty years old, and most of this proposal's structural components have direct ancestors in it.
Dependency-directed backtracking (Stallman & Sussman 1977) traced the dependency of deductions to focus search, ensuring that on finding a conflict, search retreats far enough to actually remove the inconsistency. Doyle's JTMS (1979) was the first domain-independent truth maintenance system: it records data, their justifications, and revises beliefs when assumptions change or contradictions arise — operating on the reasons for beliefs rather than their content. de Kleer's ATMS (1986) explicitly represents the assumption sets under which each fact is believed, thereby maintaining multiple mutually inconsistent contexts simultaneously and tracking minimal inconsistent environments as nogoods. Uncertainty extensions followed: probabilities on ATMS assumptions (de Kleer & Williams 1987), Dempster-Shafer belief in Doyle's TMS (Falkenhainer 1988), possibilistic logic for uncertain ATMS knowledge (Lang & Prade 1990).
