Two lessons carry beyond citation. A scaling warning: TMS design features were understood to directly determine the computational expense of relying on one, and justification-network maintenance at scale is a substantial part of why the approach lost ground to statistical methods. This is the strongest available argument that compression (§7.2) is load-bearing rather than optional. An inherited problem: Doyle observed that humans exhibit "burn-in," where long-standing beliefs come to be believed independently of their reasons, and explicitly declined to study it. That is the entrenchment ratchet (§5.5), named in 1979 and set aside.
2.2 Agent memory systems
MemGPT/Letta treats the model as an operating system managing memory across a main context, a recall store, and an archival store, with the model paging via function calls. Zep/Graphiti (Rasmussen et al. 2025) maintains a temporally-aware knowledge graph synthesizing conversational and structured data with explicit temporal validity. A-MEM (Xu et al. 2025) stores memories as structured notes, links them dynamically, and updates existing notes as new information arrives. Mem0 implements a three-tier hierarchy across user, session, and agent scopes combining vector, graph, and key-value storage. Mem-α learns memory construction via reinforcement learning — the most direct alternative to a hand-specified policy, and a baseline rather than a competitor.
Existing benchmarks: LOCOMO, LongMemEval, Deep Memory Retrieval (DMR).
The gap: these systems largely treat the graph as a persistent store of extracted facts; the evolution of memory over time is not modeled.
2.3 Knowledge editing and ripple effects
The RippleEdits benchmark (Cohen et al. 2024) evaluates the downstream consequences of a single parameter edit across categories including logical generalization, compositionality, subject aliasing, preservation, and relation specificity, finding that current methods frequently fail to keep updates consistent. That literature already treats blast radius as a measured variable: changing the country of Rome entails many follow-up changes, while updating a person's siblings is comparatively local. GradSim (Qin et al. 2024) finds gradient similarity strongly correlated with successful ripple propagation. ChainEdit pairs knowledge-graph-derived logical rules with model reasoning for chain updates; RAKEL applies graph transformers to the same problem; MQuAKE evaluates via multi-hop questions and finds degradation as logical depth increases.
This work optimizes single-edit correctness in parameters. It does not manage claim status across many edits over long horizons, which is the complement addressed here.
2.4 Self-amending systems
Suber's Nomic (1982) models a rule system in which changing the rules is a legal move, and establishes two results this proposal depends on. First, a rule designated non-amendable cannot be amended directly — it must first be transmuted into an amendable rule, which is itself a move requiring the ordinary procedure. Change of status precedes change of content, as a separate and separately recorded act. §5.7 adopts this. Second, self-amendment generically produces states in which the resulting rules are contradictory or insufficient to determine what is permitted — which are the two informative states of the evidence ledger (§5.1), reached from rule dynamics rather than from logic. Suber further observed that a program written to track the rule state and adjudicate validity would require continual modification to keep pace with the changes it tracks; §13.4 is that observation in this setting, and it remains unresolved. SoliNomic (2022) encodes the transmutation structure as tamper-evident self-modifying code, and is the nearest implemented analogue.
2.5 Contribution
Framed accurately: this is a synthesis, and the contribution is the control architecture that Dempster-Shafer, AGM belief revision, truth maintenance, and reinforcement theory form when assembled around persistent agent memory — not a set of novel mechanisms. Almost every component here has an antecedent, and the antecedents are cited above. What has not been done is the assembly, the derivation of the components from a single objective, and the specification of what the assembled system may and may not do.
Five things are new in that assembly:
Longitudinal status governance — promotion earned across contexts, demotion with deliberate threshold asymmetry, disuse decay, entrenchment management.
A two-sided evidence ledger replacing scalar confidence, preserving the distinction between unknown and contested (§5.1).
Adjudication by charge with dispositions ordered by reversibility, and a two-stage terminal path (§7.3–7.4).
Negative authority as a stated constraint on the governance layer, with the one operation that violates it isolated and bounded (§5.3, §7.2).
An objective function from which the above derive rather than being independently motivated.
3. Objective and Scope
3.1 Objective function
Maximize expected predictive performance on future queries, per unit of protected capacity and per unit of governance compute, subject to bounded degradation on held-out competence and bounded miscalibration.
Formally: maximize E[performance] / (C_protected + λ·C_governance) subject to (a) degradation on the held-out regression set ≤ δ, (b) calibration error ≤ ε.
Protected capacity is operationalized as Σ(protection weight × fan-out) over consolidated claims. This is not raw storage — it measures how much of the store is resistant to change weighted by how much depends on it, which is the quantity that actually constrains plasticity. Both factors are already computed for other purposes.
Governance compute is a first-class cost. Without it, a system can satisfy every performance and degradation constraint while becoming too expensive to run. Its inclusion is also what makes the cost hierarchy (§8.1) a derived consequence rather than an independently motivated engineering convention.
Calibration is bounded separately because accuracy does not capture it. Two systems answering equally well differ materially if one knows when it is uncertain and the other does not, and this framework computes standing everywhere without any term rewarding that standing being warranted. ε closes that gap, and §5.3 makes it load-bearing: it is the only quantity in the framework against which the governance layer's own discretion can be scored.
