On "maximize predictive performance" versus "retain contradictory models" (§6.11): these are compatible only because performance is evaluated per context. The objective is expected performance over the query distribution, and two claims with disjoint applicability domains never compete on the same query. This is not a global single model being optimized; it is a portfolio whose members are scored where they apply.
3.2 Why explicit retrieval is required
Reliability (§6.6) requires per-claim contribution estimates. The only affordable estimator is retrieval-conditioned accuracy — comparing performance on queries where a claim was retrieved against where it was not — which exists only where retrieval is explicit. Ablation and causal tracing are accurate but too expensive to run continuously. This is a consequence of needing a cheap signal, not an architectural preference.
3.3 The pretraining boundary, and a route across it
Knowledge acquired during pretraining is treated as a base the gate governs additions to but does not itself revisit. Retroactive audit requires claim decomposition and edge inference across the entire model — the same problem as §6.4 at vastly larger scale.
The cost, stated plainly:
The least-vetted knowledge in the system is what cannot be governed. Pretraining corpora contain error, duplication, and contradiction — what independence weighting exists to catch — and that portion carries no provenance.
A functional hole, not merely a scope limit. Exclusion-edge lookup is the cheapest and most-used conflict signal here, and it only fires between two claims both in the graph. When a new claim contradicts something known only in weights, there is no edge to look up, and the conflict is invisible to the gate's primary mechanism.
Route across the boundary: lazy materialization, specified in §8.3 and classified as optional. The route exists; the hole is survivable without it.
Where the boundary bites least: when the deployment target is an agent's accumulated knowledge about a user, a codebase, or an organization, little of that knowledge is pretrained, and it is also where knowledge is most recent, most uncertain, and most exposed to manipulation. The scope is most complete there — but not complete, since schema change occurs in user-preference evolution and organizational restructuring, and schema-change execution is unsolved (§13.2).
3.4 What the objective cannot price, and why it is not a discount-rate problem
Governance compute enters as pure cost, but some governance operations are investments. Abstraction spends compute now to reduce protected capacity later. Bootstrap seed edges spend manual effort once to improve every subsequent inference. Derivation-trace instrumentation costs at capture and pays at every later query. §12 sequences the build order on exactly this logic while the objective it claims to derive from cannot represent the reasoning, and a myopic optimizer against the objective as written never runs abstraction.
The obvious repair is a discount rate on future protected capacity. That repair does not fit the problem, and saying why matters more than the repair itself.
A discount rate corrects for a cost and a benefit arriving at different times in the same denomination. Abstraction's is not that case. Its compute cost is denominated in governance compute and lands in the consolidation budget; its benefit is denominated in protected capacity and lands in the retrieval budget. The two are not the same quantity at two times. They are two quantities, and the ratio in §3.1 is a single-perspective accounting that cannot represent a transfer between them.
This is the general form of a problem well understood in program evaluation: where the party bearing a cost is not the party receiving the benefit, a single aggregate figure conceals the transfer, and the standard treatment is to report the objective from more than one perspective and to make the transfer itself a measured output rather than a rounding decision.
Recommended treatment. Report the objective from two perspectives — a consolidation perspective in which governance compute is the binding cost, and a serving perspective in which protected capacity is — and report the transfer between them as a first-class quantity. Operations whose cost and benefit land in different perspectives are identified as such rather than priced into one.
This is a resolution rather than a repair, and it changes the status of the item: the claim that compression derives from the objective is not pending a better estimate of some parameter. It is false as stated, because the derivation crosses a boundary the objective does not represent. §13.10 records what would be needed to make it true.
4. Reading This Document
The architecture below is ordered by what a deployment needs first, not by how the components fit together conceptually and not by the order they should be built in.
Three tiers, then a partition.
Part I — Necessary. §5 is the cheap spine: high utility, high efficiency, low implementation cost. Most of it is bookkeeping and constraint rather than machinery, and a deployment that implements only §5 has real capability. §6 is the substrate and the status loop — high utility, materially harder. §7 is expensive and unavoidable: costly components with nothing that substitutes for them.
The partition is marked after §7. Everything before it is load-bearing; the system does not work without it.
Part II — Helpful. §8 collects structures that improve the system without being required. Each states what it buys and what its absence costs. A reader may stop at the partition and have a complete, defensible architecture.
Part III — Deployment obligations. §9 is orthogonal to the utility ordering. It is not optional-because-helpful; it is legally required in deployment and architecturally optional, which is a different axis entirely and would be misrepresented by placing it on this one.
Three cautions about this ordering.
Document order is not build order. §12 is a separate, differently-ordered sequence, and where they conflict, §12 governs construction. The shadow store (§7.1) is expensive and sits in Tier C, but it must be built third, because every measurement taken before it exists must be re-taken. Abstraction (§7.2) is likewise earlier in the build than its position here suggests.
One necessary component cannot be fully bounded. Abstraction is above the partition because unbounded growth makes it unavoidable, and it is the single operation that violates §5.3's authority constraint in a way this proposal cannot close. That is uncomfortable, it is stated here rather than left to be discovered, and §7.2 gives the bounding that is available and names the part that is not.
The tiers reflect a cost structure worth noticing. Nearly everything in §5 is a detector or a constraint — a cheap query against data already stored, or a rule about what a component may do. Nearly everything in §7 is a generator or a validator. The partition falls close to the line between mechanisms that make failure visible and mechanisms that produce something, which is consistent with the framework's orientation toward avoiding falsehood rather than acquiring truth.
PART I — NECESSARY
5. The Cheap Spine
Every item in this section is low-cost, low-difficulty, and load-bearing. Several are constraints rather than components and cost nothing to adopt beyond the discipline of adopting them.
5.1 The two-sided ledger
Scalar confidence loses the distinction that matters most: two claims at 0.5 are in entirely different situations if one has no evidence and the other has substantial evidence on both sides. The framework therefore maintains, per claim, a two-sided ledger (e⁺, e⁻) in log-likelihood-ratio units, source-attributed.
w = e⁺ − e⁻ — net weight. Which way the evidence points.
τ = e⁺ + e⁻ — resolution. How much has been spent finding out.
Low τ is a gap: nothing known. High τ with w ≈ 0 is a glut: known contested. These are Belnap's N and B, reached here from the requirements of governance rather than from four-valued logic, and both are informative states requiring different responses — a gap warrants investigation, a glut warrants scoping (§6.11) or adjudication (§7.3).
The initial state is derived rather than chosen. (0,0) is the maximum-entropy prior, and a new claim begins there.
Certainty is asymptotic in both directions. No finite evidence reaches w = ±∞, which is why log-odds is the natural coordinate and why any rule stated as a credence band is suspect: 0.5 is not a weak belief but the absence of one, and a rule that triggers there triggers at the prior.
Falsity for universal claims is binary. One valid counterexample is terminal, and no quantity of confirming instances defeats it. This does not conflict with a graded ledger, because the gradation relocates: it is not in the verdict but in whether the counterexample is admissible. All uncertainty in a terminal disposition is uncertainty about the refuting evidence, never about the inference from it. §7.4 is built on this relocation. The framework does not supply a criterion for which claims are universal, which is a gap (§13.6).
5.2 The provenance partition
τ partitions by whether a contribution's provenance terminates outside the governed system: τ = τ_ext + τ_int. Only τ_ext constitutes external constraint. τ_int records the store agreeing with itself, which is a real signal about internal coherence and is not evidence about the world.
The partition costs one flag per summand and is read by four of the framework's failure detectors (§10.A). It is the single highest-ratio addition in the document.
5.3 Negative authority
The governance layer's authority is removal-only. It may suspend, refuse, decline to promote, relax protection, void evidence, or record terminally. It may not assert, create, or corroborate.
The reason is that a layer with positive authority can manufacture the evidence that justifies its own decisions, and no external check catches this, because the manufactured evidence is internally well-formed. The framework's own derivation: if the governance layer may assert, it is a source; if it is a source, independence weighting applies to it; but the component computing independence weights is that same layer, so its weight on its own contribution is self-assigned, and self-assigned weight is not weight.
Promotion tests this constraint and passes only conditionally. On the evidence ledger, promotion is plainly subtractive: evidence arrived from outside, the layer declined to intervene, and w moved because the evidence moved it. On standing, promotion is generative: protection weight is not transferred from anywhere, has no external referent, is denominated differently from evidence with no stated exchange rate, and is issued by the layer applying a threshold the layer holds. Both descriptions are accurate, and the framework must say which object the constraint governs.
It governs both, and promotion is a bounded exception rather than a non-violation. The bounding condition is that a discretionary decision is legitimate when it is scored against a standard the deciding component declared in advance and cannot quietly revise:
Promotion asserts standing, in one currency, and the assertion is scored against ε. If protection systematically outruns what the ledger warrants, calibration degrades and the promotion threshold is demonstrably wrong. Three procedural conditions make that scoring real: ε is declared before the promotions it scores; revision of ε or of the promotion threshold is a separate act from any promotion decision; and revisions are recorded with what was adduced (§5.7).
The promotion threshold is a balance point, not a tuning parameter. Set too low, false claims acquire protection and capacity is consumed without performance bought. Set too high, true claims never earn standing, the store cannot consolidate, and every query re-derives from scratch. Both failure modes are in §3.1, and stating the threshold this way gives errors a direction: protection outrunning corroboration is one side, τ accumulating without promotion is the other, and both are visible in signals already stored.
Abstraction is the one operation this bounding does not reach (§7.2). Inferred edges may be a second (§6.4, §13.7).
5.4 Derivation-trace instrumentation
Instrument the agent to emit a trace whenever a claim is produced by reasoning over other claims. The dependency is then observed rather than inferred — the only non-inferential source of edges available, and it costs almost nothing to capture if built in from the start.
Two properties make this Tier A despite being an input to Tier B machinery. It is nearly free at capture and pays at every later query. And it cannot be retrofitted: history not instrumented is history lost, so the cost of deferring it is unbounded while the cost of adopting it is a logging call.
Mark system-generated content at the provenance layer from the same moment, and exclude it from independence counts by construction (§10.4).
5.5 Hysteresis, double jeopardy, and the entrenchment ratchet
Three cheap rules addressing one family of problems.
Hysteresis. The bar to overturn exceeds the bar to establish. Without asymmetry, a system at equilibrium oscillates whenever evidence is noisy near threshold, converting protected storage into unprotected storage. Differentiated thresholds to suppress boundary oscillation is the principle behind Schmitt triggers and switch debouncing — cited as existence proof that the technique works, not as a claim of deeper analogy.
Double jeopardy. A claim acquitted on a charge may not be re-tried on the same charge without new evidence. Without this, repeated challenge is itself a mechanism of entrenchment: survive enough attacks and a claim looks battle-tested regardless of whether anything was learned.
Survived-challenge credit decays. If surviving a challenge raises standing, each failed challenge raises the next bar regardless of whether the incumbent remains correct, so early claims with weak first challengers could become effectively unfalsifiable. This is partly desirable — resistance to revision is part of what makes structure foundational — and is a designed bias that must be named as one. Decay survived-challenge credit with time and with drift in surrounding context, so standing earned under conditions that no longer hold does not persist. Growth in that credit must be strictly sublinear: under Rubin's theorem the linear case converges to a random, path-dependent limit rather than to balance, which means linear reinforcement does not merely entrench slowly but entrenches arbitrarily.
Note that the ratchet is a predicted consequence of §5.3 rather than an independently observed pathology. Promotion asserts standing; standing compounds; a mechanism that adds standing without evidence is exactly what an authority constraint should be expected to leak into.
5.6 Constraint-capture detection
Four detectors, each a query against data already stored, all four instances of one failure. Specified in §10.A rather than duplicated here.
They are Tier A because they cost nothing beyond the ledger and the provenance partition, and because without them four of the framework's most consequential failures are silent.
5.7 Policy lineage and transmutation
Policy parameters update from consolidation history, so past outcomes recalibrate future evaluation. This is the framework's own most exposed surface, and two cheap mechanisms make it auditable.
Policy lineage. Every change to a policy parameter is recorded as an append-only entry: prior value, new value, the evidence adduced, the outcome window that evidence was drawn from, and a hash of the preceding entry. The record is not summarized and not compacted. Detection: an entry whose adduced evidence falls within an outcome window the prior value generated (§10.21).
Transmutation precedes amendment. Any change to a parameter designated non-adaptive requires a transmutation proceeding, recorded and separate from the amendment itself. The parameter cannot be adjusted in the same operation that decides it should be adjustable, and the two acts must not share a justification. Adapted from §2.4.
A parameter with high τ_int and no τ_ext has been tuned entirely against itself, which is the same diagnosis §10.20 makes about claims, applied one level up.
Both are trivial to add at the start and expensive to reconstruct later, which is why they sit here rather than with the adaptive policy layer they govern (§8.4).
5.8 The reference layer as bounded evidence
The gate must be validated against something it did not select. Reference observations enter the ledger as evidence with high but bounded weight, carrying their own provenance and their own τ, subject to the same admissibility charges as any other source. They are not exempt; they are well-attested by default.
Three consequences follow, and the third is the reason for the treatment. Bounded weight is enforceable, so repeated assertion from one reference source is one summand repeated rather than many, exactly as §6.9 treats any correlated origin. Disagreement becomes bidirectional: when a well-corroborated region of the serving graph persistently disagrees with the reference layer, that is a conflict between two evidenced positions rather than automatically the serving graph being wrong. And persistent disagreement concentrated in one reference-source cluster is an internal signal that the channel has drifted, available without external audit — where previously drift was detectable only from outside.
