6.2 What the substrate buys — a worked example
Consider a five-claim store in a codebase agent:
C1: "The auth service validates tokens against Redis."
C2: "Token validation is synchronous." (supports: C1)
C3: "Login latency is dominated by token validation." (supports: C2)
C4: "Redis runs in the same availability zone as auth." (supports: C1)
C5: "Session data is stored in Postgres." (independent of C1–C4)
A new claim arrives: C1′ — "The auth service validates tokens locally against a cached JWKS."
Without dependency edges. The system sees a claim similar to C1 and different from it. Its options are to overwrite C1, store both, or apply a recency heuristic. C2, C3, and C4 are untouched, because nothing records that they depend on C1. The store now asserts local validation and that validation is synchronous against Redis in a co-located zone — an incoherent state, silently, with no signal that anything is wrong. This is the cascade failure the §1 measurements quantify.
With dependency edges. C1′ triggers an exclusion-edge lookup against C1: fires. Blast radius is computed as the support-closure of C1 — {C2, C3, C4}, size 3, moderately connected. Regression set is constructed as the closure of claims not depending on C1 — {C5} plus whatever else the store holds independently — so C1 and its dependents are correctly excluded from validating their own replacement. C1′ must clear promotion criteria independently (§6.7), and if it does, adjudication runs on the shared domain (§7.3). On promotion, confidence propagation (§6.10) attenuates through C2, C3, and C4 rather than leaving them asserting stale premises at full weight — C4 in particular loses most of its relevance, its utilization will fall, and decay will eventually relax its protection (§6.8) without anyone having to refute it.
The difference is not that the second system is more cautious. It is that the second system can see the consequences of an update, and the first cannot see them in principle.
