6.4 Edge construction and the bootstrap problem
Edge inference has the same pathologies as claim consolidation: spurious edges (correlation read as dependency), missing edges, stale edges. A false support edge corrupts regression construction; a false exclusion edge triggers spurious demotion; a missing exclusion edge lets contradictions consolidate silently.
The bootstrap problem. The substrate requires governance (edges need status), and governance requires the substrate (status decisions read edges). Starting from pure inference means the first governance decisions are made on the least reliable edges the system will ever have, and errors made then propagate into everything built on top.
Bootstrap protocol. Begin with a seed edge set that is not inferred: derivation traces (§5.4); manually verified seeds for the store's highest-fan-out region, where errors are most consequential; and synthetic contradictions with known ground truth, used to calibrate exclusion-edge inference before it is trusted on real data. Inference extends from that seed rather than constituting the whole graph, and inferred edges are marked as such so that confidence reflects provenance.
Sources for inference, in order of preference: gradient similarity (correlated with ripple propagation, §2.3); co-retrieval statistics; ablation-measured behavioral coupling (accurate, expensive, offline only).
Edge status uses fixed thresholds rather than the adaptive policy layer. This is a deliberate simplification to keep the recursion shallow, not a derived result — it is the reason the framework does not obviously regress infinitely, and it is untested. If fixed edge thresholds prove inadequate, the termination question in §13.4 becomes live.
An unresolved authority question. Inferred support and exclusion edges are asserted by the governance layer and have no provenance terminating outside it. Under §5.3's test this makes edge inference a candidate second violation of negative authority, and arguably a more consequential one than abstraction, since edges are what propagation runs on and a false edge routes status changes to the wrong claims silently. The framework currently treats structure as categorically different from content, which may be right — a relation is not a claim — but the distinction is assumed rather than argued. §13.7.
