Demotion is comparative rather than requiring two calibrated absolute thresholds. Decay is decoupled from correctness, so obsolete claims need not be actively refuted — refutation does not scale. Fan-out is structural rather than measured.
6.6 Reliability is latent, not measured
Retrieval-conditioned accuracy is affordable but does not measure Reliability directly, and the failure is systematic in two directions. A claim rarely retrieved but always correct when retrieved scores high while contributing little. A claim retrieved constantly but sometimes omitted because adjacent claims answer instead scores lower while carrying more value.
Treat Reliability as a latent quantity with several noisy indicators: retrieval-conditioned accuracy (cheap, high-variance), sampled ablation delta (expensive, low-variance), and fan-out (free, weakly informative). Estimate it with a measurement model rather than a single statistic. Fan-out may be weakly informative because it measures a different kind of quantity from the other two rather than because it is noisy; that question belongs to the companion work on relational governance and is not settled here.
This weakens what the cheap signal must satisfy — no longer accuracy, only unbiasedness with characterizable variance — which T1.2 tests directly.
6.7 Gated promotion
A candidate accumulates Reliability across contexts with independence-weighted corroboration; promotes when the latent estimate is sufficiently precise and effective independent count suffices. The authority analysis for this operation is §5.3.
6.8 Disuse decay
Promotion and adjudication are both challenger-driven. The common case is that a claim stops mattering with nothing contradicting it. Without decay, protection accumulates monotonically until it covers enough of the store that nothing can change — the stability-plasticity problem returning by accretion rather than by any single bad decision. Stale-but-uncontested knowledge is worse than stale-and-contested, because nothing triggers review.
Track retrieval recency and frequency; gradually reduce protection weight for low-utilization claims. Not deletion — protection is relaxed, capacity is made available. Decay is slow relative to promotion, since disuse is weak evidence of irrelevance. Decay rate is weighted inversely by peak historical Reliability, and the rarity floor (§5.9) exempts flagged claims entirely.
6.9 Independence weighting
Without it, a claim repeated across correlated contexts — one source replayed, a coordinated injection, or the system encountering its own prior output — produces the same signature as genuine corroboration. The self-referential case is structurally identical to model collapse in the synthetic-data literature: any system with persistent memory whose outputs re-enter its inputs is exposed by default.
Independence is computed over provenance edges, which are themselves subject to inference error and to the same self-reference the mechanism defends against. The cheap defences are to require derivation traces to carry an external timestamp or content hash, and to mark system-generated content at the provenance layer and exclude it from independence counts by construction. Both are §5.4 and should be built in from the start.
6.10 Confidence propagation
If A supports B supports C and A weakens, C should be affected.
Δconf(child) = Δconf(parent) × conf(edge) × α, with α < 1. Propagation halts when Δ falls below threshold, bounding cost to O(k) in depth.
Propagation does not trigger demotion. It changes weight, affecting retrieval ranking, regression weighting, and future adjudication. Status changes still require §7.3. Keeping these separate prevents one weakened premise from cascading into mass demotion.
Adopt an existing TMS uncertainty formalism (probabilistic, Dempster-Shafer, or possibilistic — §2.1) rather than devising a new attenuation heuristic. Behavior under cycles in the support graph is unresolved (§13.4).
6.11 Domain-scoped coexistence
Not all contradictions require resolution. The system maintains predictive models, not truths — Newtonian and relativistic mechanics need no reconciliation to both be retained; they have different domains of predictive validity, and per §3.1 they are never scored on the same query.
On a contradiction event, check whether the exclusion edge's domain scope is non-empty: do contexts exist where both would be invoked?
Disjoint → retain both with applicability conditions. No proceeding.
Overlapping → adjudicate under §7.3, scoped to the overlap.
Maintain an explicit register of unreconciled contradictions as a first-class output.
ATMS achieved simultaneous inconsistent contexts in 1986; the contribution here is integration with lifecycle management — domain scopes derived from observed invocation patterns, never from declared scope, and updated over time. Deriving scope from declaration is exploitable: a claim could evade adjudication by acquiring a narrow self-serving domain (§10.6). The cost of that choice is §5.9's bias.
An asymmetry worth noting: abstraction (§7.2) requires scope declaration while coexistence refuses it. The two are consistent because a declared scope is checkable against evidence in the abstraction case and self-serving in the coexistence case, but the framework should state the discriminator rather than let the inconsistency stand unremarked.
7. Expensive and Unavoidable
Costly components with nothing that substitutes for them. Each states its cost plainly.
7.1 The shadow store
Deriving regression sets from the serving graph is only partially external: genuinely independent of claim status, since sets are constructed structurally rather than curated, but circular with respect to edge quality, because the same inferred edges under question determine what the set contains.
A physically separate shadow store. Maintain a second graph used exclusively for regression-set construction — never for retrieval, never for inference, never written to by the gate — refreshed periodically from external, non-agent sources: curated databases, human annotation, held-out ground truth. It implements §5.8's policy physically, and §5.8's currency and coverage obligations attach to it.
Cost: a second store, its refresh pipeline, and its source relationships. Why unavoidable: without it, every regression measurement is circular, and every measurement taken before it exists must be re-taken. This is why it is third in the build order despite sitting in Tier C (§12).
7.2 Abstractive routing
Trigger: a recurring dependency subgraph motif across multiple regions whose members show high co-retrieval.
Operation: identify the motif; synthesize a claim X summarizing its structure; redirect external edges from the motif's members to X; retain the source subgraph at full protection until the conditions below are met.
Motif recurrence is a heuristic for abstraction, not a mechanism for it. Abstraction in the full sense is latent-variable discovery, and recurring structure sometimes indicates a real higher-order concept and sometimes indicates nothing but a common template. The claim here is narrow: motif frequency is a cheap, testable proxy that identifies candidates for compression, validated by whether the compression preserves behavior. Whether the resulting nodes constitute concept formation is not established, and T2.5 tests only the compression property.
X enters as a candidate, not as an installation. X begins at (0,0) and accumulates its own ledger from its own retrievals and its own externally constrained predictions. It does not inherit the motif's accumulated evidence, because that evidence supports the motif's members, not the summary. Until X promotes on its own record it is retrievable but may not be cited as support by other claims.
Acceptance on regression performance where the motif applied is not sufficient, and an earlier form of this test was inconsistent with §5.1. X is a generalization over its members — a universal, or near enough — and universals are refuted by counterexample and not established by instances. Testing X where the motif already lived is accumulating confirming instances on exactly the domain where X is guaranteed to pass. Worse, it is silent where X does its work: X is worth having only because it applies where no motif member did, so the value of X and the risk of X are both concentrated in the extension.
X must declare its scope at synthesis, and X's ledger is scored against the declared scope rather than against its own evidence distribution. Evidence concentrated in a subregion of a wider declared scope is a gap in a nameable region, measured by the same distributional-divergence instrument §5.8 uses for reference coverage, pointed at X's evidence against X's claim.
Declaration is bounded from both directions. Over-declare and the coverage measure exposes it. Under-declare and X is retrievable only within the narrow scope it claimed, which strips the generality that justified synthesizing it.
Source retention runs until coverage, not until promotion. Sources buy the ability to construct a different summary; X promoting on the motif's home domain does not establish that X was the right generalization. Sources are retained until X's evidence covers X's declared scope, with re-derivation checks running over that interval. A failed check reverts to the source and demotes X.
Two terms the coverage bound does not capture.
Compression spends resolution at retrieval. A summary that replaces its members reduces stored structure and increases what must be supplied from context when it is retrieved — the same trade a language makes between a large vocabulary with specific definitions and a small one with high density and contextual disambiguation. Neither is better; they move cost between storage and decoding. X may satisfy coverage while requiring materially more disambiguation at retrieval than its members did, and that is a net loss the current test cannot see. Measure retrieval-time disambiguation cost before and after, and treat an increase as offsetting the capacity saved.
A single declared scope can erase scope variance among members. If the motif's members held heterogeneous scopes and X declares one, the variation is destroyed silently and X will be applied where no member applied. Compare X's declared scope against the distribution of member scopes and flag high variance; this is the abstraction-level form of imposing a global pattern on locally-scoped units, which corrupts precisely the local information that made the units distinct.
Abstraction runs as an offline batch process — knowledge-store compaction, analogous to database vacuum — never on the serving path.
What remains unbounded, and why this section sits above the partition anyway. The above bounds X's content on the scope axis. It does not bound X's selection: X was drawn from an unbounded space of possible summaries and nothing establishes it was the right member of that space, because the alternatives were never enumerated. If a mediocre-but-true X installs and the sources eventually release, a better summary Y becomes permanently unreachable, since the evidence that would have supported Y lived in the members. That is an error propagating while destroying the means of its own detection — the framework's only silent corruption mode (§10.9) — and it is why abstraction remains the sole violation of §5.3 after the fixes above.
Growth is asymptotically unbounded without compression, so the operation is necessary. It is also the operation this proposal cannot fully bound. Both are true, and §4 flags the discomfort rather than resolving it.
7.3 Adjudication by charge
Protection is a reducible weight, reduced only through defined re-evaluation. Adjudication is a proceeding rather than a threshold crossing.
Preconditions. A contradiction event is necessary but insufficient — conflict is already evidence of the incumbent's importance, so conflict alone would make protected knowledge trivially reversible by any disruptive update. The challenger must independently clear promotion criteria first. Re-evaluation runs both claims against a regression set excluding the contested claim's dependents, drawn from the shadow store (§7.1). Adjudication is by comparative advantage on the shared applicability domain; an empty shared domain means no adjudication (§6.11).
Charges. Proceedings are opened by charge, in three classes:
Class
Asserts
Against the claim
this claim has a defect
Against the evidence
this evidence should not count
Against a configuration
the arrangement that produced this is defective
The third class is doing the most work. Most consequential failures are not a claim being wrong but a process that will keep producing wrong claims, and individual adjudication cannot see those by construction.
Each charge carries its own standard of proof and its own admissible dispositions. Where the evidence establishes a defect but does not discriminate between two claims in an epistemic equivalence class, the disposition is non liquet — the proceeding closes without a finding, and neither claim's standing changes.
Dispositions are ordered by reversibility, cheapest and most recoverable first. No claim may be disposed at a rung the evidence does not reach. §5.5's hysteresis and double jeopardy govern the proceeding.
