PART II — HELPFUL
8. Optional Structures
8.1 Cost hierarchy enforcement
Each stage should be cheaper than what it gates by a wide margin: salience ≪ exclusion lookup ≪ graph-local proxy ≪ validity tracking ≪ offline episode. A stage violating this ordering should be merged into its neighbor.
The test is not "does this stage improve performance" but "does this stage change routing outcomes at all." A stage that changes no outcome is negative-value against §3.1's governance-compute term (T3.1).
This ordering governs reversible dispositions; §7.4 states the different criterion for irreversible ones.
Why optional: it is a discipline for keeping governance overhead bounded, not a mechanism the architecture requires. Absent it, governance cost grows silently and §3.1's denominator degrades — which is a real cost, and slower-acting than anything above the partition.
Open: the disposition ladder orders by reversibility and this hierarchy orders by expense. These are presented as unrelated and may be the same ordering, since recoverable operations are largely also cheap ones. If they coincide, one is derivable from the other and the framework claims two results where it has one.
