Where density is below the §10.12 threshold, high friction raises consolidation priority for that region rather than lowering it, subject to §10.12's refusal rule for high-consequence operations in the same region. A floor prevents silent starvation (§10.13).
Why optional: a simple budget scheduler with a floor works. This improves allocation and depends on T0.3 separating friction from ambiguity; if T0.3 fails, this section is discarded and the rest of the architecture is unaffected.
8.6 The special proceeding
Multi-agent and therefore outside §1.3. Specified because the disposition it governs is the one class where single-model idiosyncrasy is unrecoverable.
Five components: four limbs in two pairs drawn from a pool of at least four models of distinct training provenance, paired at random per cohort, plus one special counsel structurally separated from the pool.
No component may assert. A pair's output is a failure to sustain an objection, and the merged output of both pairs is the same. A panel producing a verdict is asserting, and four models agreeing produces an artifact internally well-formed by construction whether or not it tracks anything.
Roles are assigned by structural asymmetry, never by label. Within a pair, one limb holds the constraining role with respect to a declared product — the admissibility of the counterexample — and the assignment must derive from a real difference in what varies and on what timescale: differential instrument access, differential evidence access, or differential currency. Handing symmetric peers a role label produces a model instructed to behave like a constraining structure, with the label doing all the work. The constraining limb's position must not be movable by the generative limb.
Pairs deliberate blind and in parallel. Sequential deliberation manufactures anchoring and destroys the informativeness of inter-pair agreement.
The special counsel attacks the predicate: it argues that the admissibility question was never genuinely open. It cannot consent. Consensus is cheap among language models, and a component structurally forbidden to reach it removes the cheapest failure mode in any panel arrangement. It stands down on exhaustion, not credence — high τ with w ≈ 0 on the predicate question. A credence band around 0.5 would be the wrong coordinate: 0.5 is the prior, the rule would oppose at both 80% and 20% while standing down where least has been learned, and a scalar cannot distinguish balanced-after-investigation from not-yet-investigated. The counsel's τ threshold and effort ceiling are declared before the cohort opens and recorded; adjustment mid-cohort voids the cohort, and adjustment between cohorts is a policy change under §5.7.
The in-scope substitute. The counsel's function is available as a charge without a second model:
Proceeding without predicate. Charge against a configuration. The proceeding was opened where the admissibility question was not genuinely contested. Disposition: dismissal; the claim returns to normal standing. Aggravation: repeated instances traceable to one docketing configuration are chargeable against that configuration.
That charge is in scope and belongs with §7.3. The five-limb version is not.
8.7 The adversarial reserve
A system that consolidates from interaction has consolidation as an attack surface, and a sufficiently distributed campaign is indistinguishable from genuine corroboration by construction. Candidate defenses — source-diversity floors, temporal-spread requirements, cryptographic attribution, challenge episodes on anomalously rapid promotion — raise cost without closing the surface.
A budgeted reserve bounds the remainder. Allocate a fraction of protected capacity as an adversarial reserve — claims requiring external audit or high-confidence attestation to modify — with reserve size a deployment parameter traded against plasticity.
Why optional: the framework's actual defence against adversarial input is that content clears the same bar regardless of origin (§5.3, §6.9), and that is above the partition. The reserve bounds how much of the store manipulation can reach, which is weaker than prevention and is a deployment choice about risk appetite.
PART III — DEPLOYMENT OBLIGATIONS
9. Privacy, Retention, and Consent
Orthogonal to the utility ordering. Legally required in deployment, architecturally optional — which is a different axis, and placing it on the utility scale would misrepresent it in both directions.
The evidence ledger records provenance, retrieval statistics, and per-claim signal history. In a user-facing agent this constitutes a persistent, queryable, structured audit trail of user interactions — richer and more durable than conversation logs, because it records not only what was said but what the system inferred, when, on what basis, and how its weight changed.
This is not incidental to the design; provenance is the mechanism that makes independence weighting and adjudication possible. The framework's central capability and its central privacy exposure are the same artifact.
Retention limits on provenance, distinct from retention on claims. A claim may be worth keeping after the evidence trail that established it must be discarded, which means adjudication becomes unavailable for that claim — an explicit, documented trade rather than a silent one.
Deletion propagation. A user deletion request must remove provenance and, where a claim rests solely on deleted evidence, demote or remove the claim. Because support edges make dependents locatable, the framework can execute this correctly — deletion propagation is one place where dependency structure is a privacy asset.
Scope segregation between user-specific claims and general knowledge, so that per-user deletion does not degrade shared competence.
Consent for inference recording, not merely for content storage. Users may reasonably accept that statements are retained while objecting to a durable record of what the system concluded about them.
Auditability as a user-facing feature. The same provenance that creates the exposure also allows a system to answer "why do you believe that about me?" — the strongest available mitigation, and it should be exposed rather than merely logged.
A tension with §7.4. The terminal record retains the ground of decision permanently, and deletion propagation requires that evidence be removable. Vacatur exists precisely for the case where predicate evidence is voided, so the mechanisms are compatible in form: a deletion request voiding predicate evidence vacates the entry. What is not resolved is whether the record may retain a hash of deleted evidence sufficient to establish that vacatur was warranted, which is a jurisdiction-dependent question the framework should flag rather than answer.
10. Failure Modes
10.A Constraint capture
Four entries below are the same failure: a check has been captured by the thing it checks. The general signature is any quantity used as a check whose construction is downstream of what it checks.
The qualifier matters. A control system is supposed to affect the variable it reads — influence on the measured quantity is the mechanism, not the defect. Capture is the narrower case where the influence runs to the measurement rather than to the quantity measured.
10.19 Terminal authority. Any source treated as unrevisable creates a region evidence cannot reach. Instances: pseudo-nodes assigned a prior at or near certainty (§8.3), a reference layer treated as ground truth (§5.8), and — where a panel is used — panel consensus treated as terminal. Detection: a claim whose w has not moved despite accumulating τ. Mitigation: every source has a finite maximum weight; no source has a floor other evidence cannot overcome. §7.4's terminal record is an apparent instance and is licensed by the argument given there.
10.20 Commitment drift. Protection accumulates from reliance rather than corroboration. Utilization drives decay, decay determines protection, protection influences ranking, ranking drives utilization — so a claim acted on repeatedly gains standing with no evidence entering the loop at any point. Two claims each retrieved a thousand times, one repeatedly tested against external constraint and one never, are indistinguishable in the signal governing their protection. Distinct from 10.2, which requires evidence that evades independence weighting; this requires no evidence at all. Detection: utilization rising while τ_ext stays flat. Mitigation: cap the protection utilization alone can confer; above the cap, external resolution is required. Utilization continues to govern decay below the cap, preserving §6.8's function.
10.21 Policy recursion. Policy thresholds are tuned against outcomes those thresholds produced. Detection: a lineage entry whose adduced evidence falls within an outcome window the prior value generated (§5.7).
10.5 Reference-layer drift. The regression source becomes stale or narrows to cover only what is already protected. Detection: refresh-cadence monitoring, distributional divergence between reference and serving stores, and persistent serving-graph disagreement concentrated in one reference cluster (§5.8).
10.23 Environmental drift. (New.) Measured performance improves because the query distribution shifted, not because the store improved — or degrades for the same reason while the store is fine. Every performance figure in §3.1 is computed against a query stream the framework does not control and does not monitor, so a distributional shift is attributed to governance by default.
Distinct from the four above: the check has not been captured, it has been relocated. The store is being scored against a different population than the one it was scored against last month.
Detection: performance change accompanied by divergence between the current query distribution and a retained baseline — the §5.8 coverage instrument, pointed at queries rather than at claims. Mitigation: retain a frozen query sample and report performance against both the live stream and the frozen sample; where they diverge, the difference is the environmental component. This costs one stored sample and one extra evaluation pass.
This failure mode also weakens several experiments in §11, which compare performance across time or across systems without holding the query distribution fixed.
10.24 Fidelity failure. A stage does not run, runs on truncated input, or runs outside its declared parameters, and the resulting degradation is attributed to the policy. Distinct from 10.13, which is one cause of it; a stage can also fail fidelity through a configuration error, a dependency outage, or a silent budget cut. Detection: §5.11's execution records, compared against declared parameters. Why it matters beyond the immediate loss: every performance measurement in §11 is uninterpretable without it, because a design that was not executed and a design that does not work produce the same numbers.
10.B Remaining modes
10.1 Claim instability (highest severity). If claims cannot be individuated as stable units, there is nothing to govern. Detection: the same nominal claim yielding inconsistent effect estimates across probing methods. Tested first (T0.1).
10.2 False promotion. Correlated corroboration evading independence weighting; retrieval-conditioned accuracy confounded by co-occurring useful claims.
10.3 False demotion. A correct incumbent overturned by a well-corroborated wrong challenger. Mitigations: hysteresis; shared-domain-only adjudication; shadow-store regression sets.
10.4 Provenance self-reference. System-generated content entering the provenance graph as an apparently independent source, defeating the mechanism designed to prevent exactly that. Mitigation: mark system-generated content at the provenance layer and exclude it from independence counts by construction (§5.4).
10.6 Context spoofing. A claim acquires a narrow applicability domain to evade adjudication, or artificially diverse contexts defeat independence weighting. Mitigation: scope from observed invocation only; independence as provenance connectivity rather than surface dissimilarity.
10.7 Rare-knowledge attrition. §5.9's bias reaching safety-critical knowledge. Mitigation: rarity floor with external annotation — imperfect, since it depends on outside labeling.
10.8 Invisible pretraining conflict. Per §3.3(2). Mitigation: lazy materialization (§8.3), which is optional.
10.9 Abstraction corruption. A wrong abstraction propagates error downstream while destroying the evidence needed to detect it — the only silent corruption mode here, and the operation §5.3 independently identifies as the sole authority violation. Mitigations: scope declaration with coverage measurement; source retention until coverage; scheduled re-derivation; revert-and-demote on failure (§7.2). The selection axis remains unbounded.
10.10 Procedural leakage. Procedural content extracted as structured notes enters the declarative store and is governed by machinery that does not apply to it (§1.3). Mitigation: a procedural-content filter, unspecified.
10.11 Oscillation. Competing claims alternate near threshold. Mitigation: hysteresis; status-change frequency as a health metric.
10.12 Graph fragmentation. Sparse or uneven edges leave regions with no usable dependency structure, so regression construction and blast-radius estimation silently degrade to guesses. Mitigation: per-region density monitoring; refuse high-consequence operations in under-connected regions rather than proceeding on a degraded query. This is the first instance of §5.11's competence-boundary rule and not a special case of it.
10.13 Compute starvation. Under sustained load, budget-adaptive thresholds rise indefinitely and consolidation stops silently while the system appears healthy. Mitigation: budget floor; alarm on sustained threshold elevation.
10.14 Saturation. Protection accumulates until nothing can change, or governance cost dominates retrieval cost. Visible in the objective (§3.1).
10.15 Adversarial consolidation. Bounded rather than prevented; see §13.3.
10.22 Docket floodin
accuser (§7.4); lapse for want of prosecution; provenance-clustered failure rates chargeable against a configuration.
11. Experiments
Tier 0 carries decision boundaries; a go/no-go without a threshold is not a go/no-go. Sample sizes assume detecting a correlation of 0.6 against a null of 0.4 at 80% power, α = 0.05, requiring n ≈ 180 for paired comparisons.
11.1 One methodological requirement, stated once
A comparison across systems measures systems. Only a comparison within a standardized store measures policy.
Two systems described by the same category — "graph memory," "agent memory" — differ in extraction, chunking, retrieval, embedding, index structure, and a dozen other choices, so an observed difference in outcome may be attributable to any of them. Statistical adjustment cannot fully repair this, because many of the relevant differences are unmeasured and some are unrecognized.
The requirement follows: hold the environment constant and let only the tested variable vary. Where that is impossible, the experiment answers a weaker question and should say which.
This applies unevenly below and the unevenness should be visible. T2.6 satisfies it — paired on the same store under two policies. T2.1 does not, and cannot: it compares this pipeline against Zep, Letta, A-MEM and Mem-α, which differ in every dimension above. T2.1 therefore establishes that this system outperforms those systems on those benchmarks, which is worth knowing and is not evidence that the governance policy is what did it. Wherever feasible, cross-system comparisons should be paired with a within-store ablation that isolates the same variable.
The same discipline applies to the query distribution, per §10.23: a comparison across time with an unmonitored query stream has an uncontrolled variable in it.
