11.3 Two limitations of the per-stage test
T3.1 disables one stage at a time and measures whether routing outcomes change. Two things this does not establish.
It measures marginal contribution holding order fixed. If governance operations interact — if abstraction run before a promotion cycle produces a different store than the same abstraction run after — then every T3.1 result is conditional on an ordering nobody tested. T3.3 is the cheapest available check and it should run before T3.1's results are used to delete anything.
Average share is the wrong denominator. A stage's value is the outcomes it produces that would not otherwise occur, per unit of what it costs — not its fraction of total performance. The reformulation above is a reporting change, not a new experiment, and it is what §3.1's governance-compute term actually requires.
11.4 A health metric the objective does not contain
§3.1 measures predictive performance per unit of capacity. Nothing measures how quickly a wrong claim is corrected once the evidence to correct it has arrived.
Correction latency: elapsed time, or number of retrievals, between refuting evidence entering the ledger and the claim's disposition changing.
This is arguably the quantity the architecture exists to minimize. A store that answers well and corrects slowly is a store whose errors persist through everything built on them during the interval, and §3.1 registers that as a small accuracy cost at the moment of measurement rather than as the compounding structural cost it is.
The measure is free — both timestamps are already recorded — and it separates two failures the objective conflates: a system that never receives correcting evidence, and one that receives it and does not act. It should be reported per disposition class, since a long latency on terminal disposition is deliberate (§7.4) and a long latency on demotion is not.
12. Build Order
Document order is not build order. §4 orders by what a deployment needs; this orders by what must exist before what else can be measured. Where they conflict, this governs construction.
Tier 0 — existing models, no new architecture. Go/no-go on premises.
T0.1 first (largest risk, cheapest test, hard stop), then T0.2, T0.3, T0.4.
Tier 1 — graph and vector substrate.
Provenance store with typed, confidence-weighted edges. Build first; edge semantics are the one design error not swappable later. Instrument derivation traces from day one (§5.4) — observed dependencies are worth more than any inference method, and retrofitting loses all history. Mark system-generated content at the provenance layer from the start.
Bootstrap seed edges (§6.4).
Shadow store (§7.1). Build alongside the serving graph, not after — regression validation without it is circular, and every measurement taken before it exists must be re-taken. This is the clearest case where build order and document order diverge.
Two-sided ledger with provenance partition (§5.1–5.2). Cheap, and every later detector reads it.
Retain a frozen query sample (§10.23). Costs almost nothing at the start; cannot be reconstructed later.
Reliability measurement model (§6.6), gated by T1.2.
Independence weighting via provenance clustering (§6.9, T1.3).
Salience estimator (§5.10), fitting α, β, γ by inverse-variance weighting against Tier 0 outcomes.
Confidence propagation (§6.10), adopting an existing formalism.
Offline abstraction as a compaction process (§7.2). Earlier than its cost suggests: growth is asymptotically unbounded, the mechanism must exist before the store is large, and running it offline from the start avoids retrofitting a vacuum process onto a saturated graph.
Tier 2 — status dynamics.
11. Gated promotion with disuse decay and the rarity floor. Build decay in from the start; the rarity floor must exist before decay does or rare knowledge is lost during the interval.
12. Policy lineage and transmutation (§5.7). Trivial at the start, expensive to reconstruct, and must precede any adaptive policy layer.
13. Offline consolidation via a replica that periodically syncs to serving copies.
14. Baseline comparison (T2.1) as soon as the pipeline is minimally functional — the fastest route to discovering which parts of this are already solved elsewhere. Pair with a within-store ablation per §11.1.
15. Adjudication by charge with shadow-store regression (§7.3).
16. Domain-scoped coexistence (§6.11).
17. Terminal docket and record (§7.4), with T2.8 before any terminal disposition is executed.
18. Sequence check (T3.3) before per-stage deletion decisions.
19. Optional structures from §8, in whatever order the deployment's constraints suggest.
A methodological caution about this sequence. It commits to one configuration of one policy and then measures it. That is efficient and it is also the failure it warns against elsewhere: designating an optimal pathway at the outset substitutes one untested design philosophy for another, and a single-arm build cannot distinguish "this works" from "this is the only thing tried." Where budget permits, at least two defensible configurations should be carried through Tier 2 in parallel — most cheaply as two policy parameterizations over one substrate, which costs a second policy layer rather than a second system.
A test that partially closes the weight-space gap. A graph-based system has no distributed superposition and therefore no catastrophic forgetting to solve — it can validate governance logic but not that the policy addresses weight-space forgetting. Partial closure: let the gate decide which updates are applied to weights via low-rank adapters, then measure weight-space forgetting with and without gate governance. Buildable at late Tier 2.
13. Open Problems Blocking Completion
The architecture is buildable. The problems below are not solved, and none is solved by having been named precisely.
13.1 Claim individuation
Everything above assumes stable claim objects. T0.1 tests whether they exist and at what granularity. A coarse result changes the unit of governance from claim to cluster, which weakens what an exclusion edge means and makes adjudication harder to scope, and the proposal has no worked version at cluster granularity.
A narrower question is deferred rather than answered here. T0.1 tests whether claims individuate by effect. Whether they also individuate by constitution, and whether the two pick out the same units, requires comparing claims to each other — which is the companion work on relational governance, not something this paper can measure. If the two do not coincide, this proposal has two units of governance where it assumes one, since Reliability and comparative advantage are effect quantities while exclusion edges and blast radius are structural.
A coarse result need not be fatal: a coarser regime may have its own effective variables that are objectively meaningful rather than degraded versions of finer ones, in which case T0.1 determines which unit governance runs on rather than whether it runs. That reframing is available and is also the most abusable move in this document, because it converts a falsification into a redescription. It counts only if the cluster-granularity version of exclusion is written and says what exclusion means between clusters. Until then it is a rescue and is labelled one here in advance.
13.2 Mutual execution
Detecting that a candidate requires structural reorganization is implementable. Performing it is a search over revised structures and is unsolved. The framework's central diagnostic claim (§1.1) therefore identifies a failure mode it cannot repair, only refuse to compound.
13.3 Adversarial consolidation is bounded, not prevented
An adversary is a source that cannot be refused and cannot be verified, which is structurally the same problem as the reference layer and as source identity generally. The framework's defence was never authentication; it is that content clears the same bar regardless of origin. What remains open is magnitude, not mechanism. §8.7 bounds the remainder, weakly.
13.4 Recursion termination
Policy parameters adapt from history (§8.4). Edge status uses fixed thresholds specifically to stop the regress (§6.4), but that is a simplification rather than a result, and it is untested. Confidence propagation under cycles is unresolved. §2.4 notes the same problem observed in self-amending rule systems four decades ago without resolution.
13.5 No convergence results
Nothing here establishes that the Reliability estimate converges, that hysteresis stabilizes rather than merely slowing oscillation, that blast radius correlates with required restructuring, or that comparative adjudication converges rather than cycling among near-equivalent claims.
The tractable next step is to state the assumptions a proof would need: under (a) evidence arriving as an ergodic process, (b) unbiased independence weighting, (c) hysteresis thresholds bounded away from zero, and (d) a finite claim space, does comparative adjudication converge? Making the assumption set explicit converts a vague gap into a specific open question, and would reveal quickly whether any assumption is one this architecture cannot satisfy.
13.6 The universality criterion
§5.1's binary/graded split is load-bearing — it generates the two-bucket model, the relocation of gradation to admissibility, and the entire terminal path — and the framework supplies no criterion for which claims are universal. This is not independent of §13.1: if claims individuate only at cluster granularity, "universal" may not be well-defined at the unit of governance.
13.7 What counts as an authority violation
§5.3 constrains the governance layer to removal-only authority and identifies abstraction as the sole violation. That identification is only as good as the declaration of what the layer produces, and two cases remain unsettled. Promotion asserts standing and is bounded by ε, which is a resolution rather than a dismissal. Inferred edges are asserted with no external provenance and are currently treated as categorically different from content because a relation is not a claim — which may be right and is assumed rather than argued.
13.8 Stated versus operative ground
Per §7.4. Where language models act as deciding components, the framework's records preserve justifications offered rather than justifications operating, and every proceedings mechanism here rests on records of reasons. Whether this admits any mechanism at all, or is a permanent limitation on using such models as adjudicating components, is open. It is arguably more load-bearing than §13.2.
The general form of the problem is stronger than the observed instance. A rendered account of a process is not the process. In human cognition the point is demonstrable rather than merely arguable: the sensory rendering of thought — inner speech, mental imagery — is dispensable, since cognition proceeds without it in individuals who do not produce it, and what is dispensable was not the mechanism. The rendering is downstream output, and the intuition that it constitutes the thinking is an artifact of living inside the display.
The design consequence is a preference rather than a mechanism: where behavioral and stated evidence are both available, prefer behavioral. This framework does not consistently apply it. Reliability is behavioral; charges rest on stated grounds. That asymmetry is not defended anywhere and should be, or removed.
13.9 Procedural filtering
The declarative scope is operationally leaky (§1.3), and no filter is specified for detecting procedural content arriving as structured notes.
13.10 The objective does not span the architecture
§3.4 establishes that the objective cannot price operations whose cost and benefit land in different denominations, and recommends dual-perspective reporting rather than a discount rate. That is a treatment, not a solution. A single objective that spans consolidation cost and serving benefit would require a stated exchange rate between governance compute and protected capacity, and no principled basis for one is offered here. Until then, the claim that compression derives from the objective is withdrawn rather than deferred.
13.11 Groundedness does not propagate
Confidence propagates along support edges (§6.10). Groundedness does not, and nothing in this proposal says whether it should.
If A carries external evidence and B depends on A, B's relation to external constraint is undefined. Two claims with identical ledgers — both τ_ext = 0, both promoted on internal corroboration — are treated identically whether one sits adjacent to a heavily-grounded region and the other sits in a wholly internal one. That is almost certainly wrong, since dependence on a grounded claim is some relation to the world, and it may be the difference between a store organized around external constraint and one that has drifted free of it while satisfying every check.
The obvious measure — path distance from a claim to the nearest node carrying τ_ext — is cheap and already computable. What is not established is whether groundedness attenuates along edges, at what rate, or whether it propagates at all rather than terminating at the node that received it.
